1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,707
The new valve is working.

2
00:00:01,916 --> 00:00:06,711
What happens next? The care team watches
the heart rhythm, checks the site where

3
00:00:06,734 --> 00:00:11,459
the catheter went in, and uses an
ultrasound scan to assess the new valve.

4
00:00:12,063 --> 00:00:15,255
These early checks guide the next part of
recovery.

5
00:00:15,638 --> 00:00:19,760
In this example, the catheter reached the
heart through an artery in the groin.

6
00:00:20,201 --> 00:00:23,672
The team checks the small covered wound
for bleeding or swelling.

7
00:00:23,997 --> 00:00:27,109
It also monitors heart rhythm and blood
pressure.

8
00:00:27,550 --> 00:00:32,473
How long the patient stays depends on
recovery, the access route, and whether

9
00:00:32,531 --> 00:00:34,539
the team finds any problems.

10
00:00:35,352 --> 00:00:39,288
The new valve sits close to part of the
heart’s electrical pathway.

11
00:00:39,729 --> 00:00:43,804
Some people develop a change in the way
electrical signals travel after

12
00:00:43,885 --> 00:00:49,284
TAVI. If that happens, the team may
monitor them for longer.

13
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:54,253
Some people need a permanent pacemaker to
help maintain a reliable rhythm.

14
00:00:54,764 --> 00:01:00,127
An echocardiogram uses ultrasound to
assess how the replacement valve opens

15
00:01:00,185 --> 00:01:02,972
and closes and how blood moves through it.

16
00:01:03,576 --> 00:01:07,662
The team can also check whether any blood
leaks around the edge of the valve.

17
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:12,852
These findings, together with the other
checks, inform the recovery plan.

18
00:01:13,549 --> 00:01:18,215
Before the patient goes home, the team
explains how to care for the wound, build

19
00:01:18,274 --> 00:01:21,733
up activity, and follow the prescribed
medicine plan.

20
00:01:22,244 --> 00:01:26,958
It also explains whom to contact with
concerns and when the next check is due.

21
00:01:27,399 --> 00:01:31,520
The details can vary with other conditions
and how the procedure went.

22
00:01:32,124 --> 00:01:36,687
The discharge instructions explain which
warning signs need urgent attention.

23
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:42,120
Severe bleeding from the access site or
possible signs of stroke need emergency

24
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:46,915
help. Stroke signs can include a drooping
face, sudden arm

25
00:01:46,984 --> 00:01:49,388
weakness, or speech difficulty.

26
00:01:49,898 --> 00:01:53,683
Call local emergency services immediately
if these occur.

27
00:01:54,124 --> 00:01:58,443
Follow-up helps the team see how the
patient feels and how the valve is working

28
00:01:58,501 --> 00:02:03,853
over time. The team may review symptoms,
medicines and rhythm, and arrange a

29
00:02:03,946 --> 00:02:09,310
repeat ultrasound. Before the patient
leaves hospital, it is useful to know who

30
00:02:09,356 --> 00:02:12,630
will arrange follow-up and whom to call
with questions.
